+### 6.1 Update check
+
+The Android APK, Windows and Linux builds can look for a newer version; the Google Play bundle has no check, the store updates it. It is not part of the
+protocol: the check talks only to the publisher's website, whose address (`FsUpdateUrl`) and Ed25519 public key (`FsUpdateKey`) are chosen at build time
+(`v2/Directory.Build.props`, overridden by `v2/freespeech.defaults.props`). A build where `FsUpdateUrl` is empty, as every custom build is by default, has
+no check and no button.
+
+- The website serves `update/latest.json` and its detached signature `update/latest.json.sig` (base64 Ed25519 over the exact bytes of the file).
+  `scripts/make-update-manifest.ps1` writes both from `v2/version.props`; the signing key stays outside the repository
+  (`%USERPROFILE%\.freespeech\update-signing.key`).
+- The manifest holds `format` (1), `published` (date), `programs` (`android-apk` with `version`, `build`, `date`; `windows` and `linux` with `version`, `date`)
+  and `download` (an `https` address with `{lang}`). The client refuses a manifest whose signature does not verify, that is more than 400 days old, whose
+  `download` is not `https`, or that lacks its program.
+- A version is available when it is higher than the installed one, or equal with a higher build number (Android). Nothing is downloaded or installed:
+  the client only opens the download page, where the SHA-256 fingerprints are.
+- The check runs when the user presses "Check for updates" in Settings, or at start-up when the user turned that on (off by default: each check tells the
+  website that someone runs the application). A version found at start-up is announced once by a dialog.
+- Code: `FreeSpeech.Client.Update.UpdateChecker`; tests in `UpdateCheckerTests`.
+
